  "description": "A quick, practical guide to Consolidation: Cementing Memories.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "A fresh memory is fragile. Disturb it within hours and it can vanish like footprints in wet sand.",
      "visual": "🏖️"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Consolidation is the process of stabilizing a memory after initial encoding—turning temporary into permanent.",
      "visual": "🧱"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "During sleep, the hippocampus replays the day's events to the cortex, strengthening synaptic connections.",
      "visual": "🌙"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "This replay happens during slow-wave sleep, which is why all-nighters sabotage learning.",
      "visual": "📉"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "Even a 20-minute nap after studying improves memory retention measurably in experiments.",
      "visual": "😴"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "Sleep isn't downtime. It's when your brain saves its work.",
      "visual": "💾"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "When does memory consolidation primarily occur?",
    "options": [
      "During slow-wave sleep",
      "While actively studying",
      "During intense exercise"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
